Chapter: LirLoadCross

 The constructor-pattern memory read: `LiLoad`, the last per-construct arm of
 the ARM64 LIR selector (LirRetarget step 4i). A `when s is Circle (r) -> ...`
 lowers to a load of the variant tag from `[sv + 0]`, a compare against the
 constructor's tag, a branch, and then one load per bound field at its packed
 offset and width. Everything but the loads was already whitelisted by steps 4e
 and 4g, so this file is what the last arm buys.

 Pinned by ANSWERS, because the two lanes reach them by different instructions
 and the answers are what must agree. x86-64 selects these through
 `lir-sel-load`; the ARM64 plug selects them through `a64-lir-emit-load`; the
 tree emitter behind both would also answer correctly, which is why the
 sensitivity run rather than the green is what proves the LIR path claimed them.

 **A constructor APPLICATION is not lowerable and a constructor PATTERN is.**
 The lowering declines `Circle 5`, so every function here takes its variant as a
 parameter and `opening` builds the values on the tree path. That split is not
 an accident of the test: it is what makes these functions reachable by the
 selector at all.

Section: The eight-byte shapes

 A variant whose every field is eight bytes wide, which is what the first
 increment admits: pointers, unbounded integers and sum payloads are all width
 eight, and `arm64-ldr` covers them with no new encoding.

 `Dot` carries no fields, so its arm tests the tag and loads nothing. `Rect`
 carries two, so the second sits at offset 16 and is the only shape here that
 reads past the first field slot.

  Shape =
   | Circle (Integer)
   | Rect (Integer) (Integer)
   | Dot

 Every arm reads a field, and the nullary arm reads none. A wrong tag load
 sends every call to the wrong arm; a wrong field offset answers a neighbour.

  sh-area : Shape -> Integer
  sh-area (s) =
   when s
    is Circle (r) -> r * r * 3
    is Rect (w) (h) -> w * h
    is Dot -> 0

 The first field alone, from two different constructors, so offset 8 is read
 under two different tags.

  sh-first : Shape -> Integer
  sh-first (s) =
   when s
    is Circle (r) -> r
    is Rect (w) (h) -> w
    is Dot -> 0

 The second field, at offset 16. This is the row that separates a load whose
 offset is honoured from one that always reads the first slot: `sh-first` and
 `sh-second` on the same `Rect` must disagree.

  sh-second : Shape -> Integer
  sh-second (s) =
   when s
    is Rect (w) (h) -> h
    is otherwise -> 0

 A wildcard arm rather than an exhaustive constructor list, and a bound field
 used twice in the arm body, so the loaded vreg outlives its defining
 instruction.

  sh-twice : Shape -> Integer
  sh-twice (s) =
   when s
    is Circle (r) -> r + r
    is otherwise -> 1

 Both fields of one constructor in one expression, so two loads are live at
 once and the allocator has to keep them apart.

  sh-both : Shape -> Integer
  sh-both (s) =
   when s
    is Rect (w) (h) -> w * 100 + h
    is otherwise -> 2

Section: The narrow shapes, which must decline

 A field narrower than eight bytes is NOT whitelisted in this increment: the
 encoder has `arm64-ldr` (8), `arm64-ldrb` (1 unsigned) and `arm64-ldr-w` (4
 zero-extending) and lacks every sign-extending narrow form, so admitting
 narrow widths is a separate change with its own encodings. These two functions
 exist to prove the gate declines rather than emitting an eight-byte load over
 a one-byte field.

 They discriminate because the two bytes are ADJACENT. Fields pack widest
 first, so `Bytes`' two one-byte fields land at offsets 8 and 9. An eight-byte
 load at offset 8 would answer `a + b * 256`, and one at offset 9 would answer
 the same thing again, because `arm64-ldr`'s unsigned form scales its immediate
 by eight and 9/8 is 1. So a gate that let these through changes both answers,
 and `pk-lo 7 3` would read 775 instead of 7.

  Packed =
   | Bytes (Integer between 0 and 255) (Integer between 0 and 255)
   | Big (Integer)

  pk-lo : Packed -> Integer
  pk-lo (p) =
   when p
    is Bytes (a) (b) -> a
    is Big (n) -> n

  pk-hi : Packed -> Integer
  pk-hi (p) =
   when p
    is Bytes (a) (b) -> b
    is Big (n) -> n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (show (sh-area (Circle 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-area (Rect 6 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-area Dot))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-first (Circle 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-first (Rect 6 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-first Dot))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-second (Rect 6 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-second (Circle 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-twice (Circle 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-twice (Rect 6 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-both (Rect 6 7)))
   print-line-uni (show (sh-both (Circle 5)))
   print-line-uni (show (pk-lo (Bytes 7 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (pk-hi (Bytes 7 3)))
   print-line-uni (show (pk-lo (Big 41)))
  end
